  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-130236",
    "knowledge_point_id": "215130d1-96ea-5f88-8de0-d73758ea8344",
    "knowledge_point": "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval Using the Complement",
    "problem": "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{2}{3}),$ compute $P(X\\ge 6)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places.",
    "worked_solution": "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{2}{3}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-1}(\\frac{2}{3}), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 6.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & =1-P(X<6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0}(\\frac{2}{3})+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}(\\frac{2}{3})+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}(\\frac{2}{3})+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}(\\frac{2}{3})+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.9959.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & =1-P(X<6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.9959 \\\\  & =0.0041.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
